Adopt two models.

**(b) lsq as today's line now.** It costs nothing extra: 99 s a run against 115, and 3552 search evaluations against 3990; under one load, 3.4-8.1 ms an evaluation against 4.0-7.6. On the 77-run screening set it changes:
- kept truths: 60/66 → 65/66;
- reach (each model against its own truth reference): 58/63 → 65/66;
- worst extent: +42 % → +16 % (90th percentile +290 % → +232 %);
- thinnest extent: +20 % → +1 %;
- axis: 6.8 → 4.3 deg (90th percentile 79 → 32 deg);
- clean anisotropy read ÷ true: 0.74 → 0.95; m-3m 0.11 → 0.88;
- false spheres: 3 → 2 of 11. The two small clean spheres are now rejected; the two kept are a texture row and a size-distribution row.

**(c) WPPM (the exact line, here the engine's own WPPM profile) for the search itself** wherever the solid must be told apart or accuracy matters. The cost is 2.2x: 254 s a run and 325 min a screening, against 115 s and 147 min; P-1 takes 953 s a run; an evaluation costs 2.4-13x more, growing with the member count. In return, on the 77-run set:
- 66/66 kept;
- worst extent +4 %, thinnest +0 %, axis 1.6 deg (90th percentile 14 deg);
- clean truths: worst +2 %, axis 0.9 deg, anisotropy 0.99, and 0.92-1.09 in every Laue class;
- solids: 11/11 in the right family by chi2 and 10/11 by BIC, against lor's 6/11 and 7/11 and lsq's 10/11 and 8/11.

**What WPPM costs besides time:** model errors are read as shape more readily. All 6 unbounded results are error rows (texture 3, size distribution 2, impurity 1), and a 4.4 nm sphere with a size distribution keeps a spheroid at dBIC -3483. Reach is 62/66; 3 of the 4 misses are error rows, the clean one is at delta 6.6.

**Rejected:**
- (a) changes the scale, not the shape;
- the moments TCH is slightly worse than lsq;
- the lsq-search / WPPM-end hybrid at 181 s a run inherits lsq's wrong basins (90th percentile +334 %).

Adopt two models. Costs are now measured under one load, as CPU time of the isotropic fit + search + report, on the 22 sub-screen rows (2 per Laue class, the screening set's class weights).

**(b) lsq as today's line now.** It costs what today costs: 0.99x lor CPU (per row 0.63-1.49), 48 s a run, with 6 % fewer evaluations (5710 against 6066). It is not cheaper, as the old '99 s against 115' suggested. On the 77-run screening set:
- kept truths: 60/66 → 65/66;
- reach: 58/63 → 65/66;
- worst extent: +42 % → +16 % (90th +290 % → +232 %);
- thinnest extent: +20 % → +1 %;
- axis: 6.8 → 4.3 deg (90th 79 → 32);
- anisotropy on the 28 clean rows every model kept: median 0.74 → 0.96, median |ln ratio| 0.33 → 0.09, rows outside 0.9-1.1: 21 → 14 of 28;
- false spheres: 3 → 2 of 11;
- solid families, expected margin on exact data (right / within ±3 / wrong): 4/3/4 → 8/1/2.

What it leaves: a 2.5-8 % line error. It makes two families wrong in expectation: cylinder-P6m-on goes to the ellipsoid by 8.9 and ellcyl-P6m-generic by about 11.

**(c) WPPM (the exact line) for the search itself** where the solid must be told apart or accuracy matters. It costs 2.88x today's CPU (per row 1.2-4.8x; triclinic 3.6x, 572 s a run against 158). The set is estimated at 179 min CPU against 62. The old '2.2x' compared walls under different loads. In return, on the 77-run set:
- 66/66 kept;
- worst extent +4 %, thinnest +0 %, axis 1.6 deg;
- anisotropy on the common clean rows: median 1.00, |ln| 0.02; still 6 of 28 rows outside 0.9-1.1 (0.70-1.72);
- solid families: never wrong in expectation (8/3/0);
- noisy cross-solid fits at margin 3: 9 right family, 2 undetermined, 0 wrong (lsq 9/1/1, lor 4/4/3).

**What WPPM costs besides time:** model errors are read as shape more readily. All 6 unbounded results are on error rows, the size-distribution sphere keeps a spheroid at dBIC -3483, and reach is 62/66.

**Rejected:**
- (a) the breadth convention: it rescales the size only;
- mom: its extents equal lsq's, but its fit, bias, reach and tail are worse;
- the lsq-search / WPPM-end hybrid: it inherits lsq's basins.
